\begin{abstract}
We introduce Lindblad Multiproduct Formulas: a quantum error mitigation technique that uses two-dimensional tensor networks contracted with loop-corrected belief propagation. The quantities required to implement the error mitigation scheme that are evaluated with tensor networks can be less computationally expensive to calculate than the expectation values themselves, thus allowing for the possibility of applying our method to certain systems for which tensor network methods may struggle to calculate the observable quantities of interest. The workflow incorporates Clifford rescaling techniques and outputs an estimated error bar. We apply our method to a model of two-dimensional discrete time crystals studied previously and implement it on $65$ qubits arranged in a $3\!\times\!3$ heavy-hexagonal topology on the quantum computer \texttt{ibm\char`_basquecountry}. We show that a GPU implementation of the classical part of our workflow achieves a speedup of up to $5.6\times$. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Estimating expectation values of observable operators after the application of noiseless, unitary quantum circuits is a central challenge of quantum computing, with applications in several areas including many-body physics \cite{switzer2026realization, kiss2026neutrino, cobos2025real}, partial differential equations \cite{bravyi2026quantum, ameri2026quantum} and the simulation of photonic systems \cite{tirado2026utility}. Various techniques have been proposed to obtain these estimates by mitigating errors on noisy quantum circuits \cite{temme2017error, eddins2026computing, kim2023evidence}. One of the main candidates to implement reliable error mitigation is probabilistic error cancellation (PEC). While PEC has been demonstrated on quantum hardware \cite{berg2022probabilistic}, scaling this method to larger and deeper circuits is challenging due to the increasing number of samples required.\\

\noindent Other candidates for scalable error mitigation include zero noise extrapolation (ZNE) with noise amplification implemented via probabilistic error amplification (PEA) \cite{kim2023evidence}. While this method can often produce impressive results on large and deep circuits since the sampling cost is significantly lower than PEC, challenges remain such as a bias in the fit of expectation values vs. the noise rescaling parameter. 
\\

\noindent At the same time, classical simulation techniques are rapidly improving, particularly those based on tensor networks \cite{tindall2023gauging, tindall2026contracting, evenbly2026loop}. In this work, we investigate whether an error mitigation workflow can be designed using state-of-the-art high-dimensional tensor network techniques, such that new advancements in these classical methods could be incorporated directly into a quantum error mitigation workflow in order to increase its accuracy and reliability. To do this, we use ideas from dynamic multiproduct formulas \cite{robertson2025tensor}. It is worth mentioning that previous work has also used tensor networks in an error mitigation workflow \cite{filippov2023scalable} tailored to mitigating one-dimensional quantum circuits. The tensor network algorithm in both \cite{robertson2025tensor} and \cite{filippov2023scalable} relied on a particular contraction path from \cite{schollwock2011density} based on the Heisenberg picture of quantum mechanics, which is referred to as a ``middle-out contraction'' path. Applying a generalization of this approach to a two-dimensional circuit is a promising direction but may require a significant classical memory overhead. \\

\noindent In this work, we sidestep this issue such that our algorithm does not rely on a particular contraction path. In particular, we use a state-of-the-art tensor network technique based on loop-corrected belief propagation \cite{tindall2023gauging, evenbly2026loop, rudolph2025simulating} and we implement it directly (i.e., without building a customized contraction path), thus opening up the possibility for new advances in this field \cite{tindall2026contracting} to be incorporated into this error mitigation workflow. We introduce Lindblad multiproduct formulas (L-MPF), the goal of which is to take a linear combination of noisy expectation values $\sum\limits_i^kc_i\langle O \rangle _{\lambda_i}$, where $c_i$ are calculated classically using tensor networks and the expectation values $\langle O \rangle _{\lambda_i}$ are calculated on a quantum computer. The L-MPF workflow is outlined in Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}. In general, the different expectation values $\langle O \rangle_{\lambda_i}$ can be obtained using PEA. We also introduce a new method which we call \textit{ergodic amplification} which has a significantly reduced sampling cost as compared to PEA. We ran our quantum experiments with both PEA and ergodic amplification in order to compare them. We find that ergodic amplification turns out to be particularly well suited to our experiments applied to the model studied in \cite{switzer2026realization}. We discuss how one could apply ergodic amplification to other classes of quantum circuits, although we leave that more general study for future work. We show that, when applied to the two-dimensional discrete time crystal studied previously in \cite{switzer2026realization}, the L-MPF workflow can produce more accurate estimates for the noiseless observable than either tensor networks or the noisy quantum computer alone. This is despite the following apparent paradox: if the problem of interest is hard to simulate classically, why doesn't the truncation error from the tensor network part of the workflow induce an error on the mitigated observable that is as large as a purely classical workflow? Loosely speaking, the resolution to this problem is that, while expectation values of observables can be difficult to evaluate with purely classical methods, the quantities required to implement error mitigation can be relatively easily evaluated with tensor networks, at least for the simulation problems that we tested the method on in this work. This statement is independent of whether a middle-out contraction path is used or not. We discuss this point in detail in the main text.\\

\noindent Previous large-scale experiments  on quantum computers have used rescaling techniques to estimate noiseless expectation values from noisy ones \cite{switzer2026realization, google2025observation}. These methods work as follows: for a given model there often exists a set of parameters for which classical simulations can calculate expectation values exactly (e.g., if the parameters are such that the resulting circuit is a Clifford circuit). We denote by $\langle O^*\rangle$ the expectation values that can be obtained exactly at these points in parameter space. By comparing the measured expectation value $\langle O^*\rangle_{\text{noisy}}$ with the exact value $\langle O^*\rangle_{\text{exact}}$ obtained classically, one defines a rescaling factor $d = \frac{\langle O^*\rangle_{\text{exact}}}{\langle O^*\rangle_{\text{noisy}}}$. Expectation values measured at other parameters for which there is no exact classical reference are then rescaled to obtain an estimate of the noiseless value by taking $\langle O\rangle_{\text{noiseless estimate}} = d \langle O\rangle_{\text{noisy}}$. While such rescaling methods can work very well when there is a reliable benchmark to compare against, interpreting the output presents challenges in the absence of error bars and a reliable classical benchmark. Here we incorporate these rescaling techniques into the L-MPF workflow, thus allowing us to combine this powerful rescaling approach with an (albeit heuristic) error bar that the L-MPF method provides.\\

\noindent This work is structured as follows: in section \ref{sec:background} we recall some relevant background material on discrete time crystals and multiproduct formulas. We also introduce the concept of \textit{ergodic amplification} discussed above. In section \ref{sec:l_mpf_alg} we outline the L-MPF algorithm which is the subject of this work and we discuss the two-dimensional tensor network techniques that the method relies on. We discuss the propagation of errors in our algorithm and explain how the classical tensor network calculations can mitigate the results from the quantum computer. In section \ref{sec:results} we present our results that combine the classical simulations based on contracting two-dimensional tensor networks with loop-corrected belief propagation and the quantum simulations implemented on \texttt{ibm\char`_basquecountry}. We conclude in section \ref{sec:discussion}.

